# Rosetta dectest per-line skips (RosettaHarnessAlignmentWorkOrder.md §4.3).
# Every port reads this file; no port keeps its own copy.
#
#   =<tag>  <reason>                  defines a reason once
#   line    <tag>  <exact line>       skips the case whose trimmed line text matches
#   attr    <tag>  <ops>  <attribute> skips lines of the comma-separated ops that
#                                     carry the attribute token
#
# Fields are tab-separated (no corpus line contains a tab). Every entry must
# match at least one corpus line (checked by rosetta/check_rosetta.py).
# Transcribed from Swift's RosettaDectestSkips.swift on 2026-11-12.

=abs-sign-bit	GDAS abs quiets a signaling NaN and keeps a NaN's sign; IEEE 754-2019 abs is a sign-bit operation (clears the sign, never signals)
=minus-sign-bit	GDAS minus is 0 - x (quiets a signaling NaN, keeps a NaN's sign, 0 - 0 is +0); IEEE 754-2019 negate is a sign-bit operation (flips the sign, never signals)
=tosci-syntax	toSci conversion-syntax: dectest reports Conversion_syntax where the core parse differs
=minmax-snan	max/min/maxmag/minmag *Number with a signaling NaN: dectest's pre-2019 file still quiets a signaling NaN to NaN, but 754-2019 maxNumber/minNumber returns the number (+ invalid)
=remainder-exact	remainder/remaindernear: IEEE 754-2019 §5.3.1 exact; GDAS signals Division_impossible (RemainderExactWorkOrder.md RX-1/RX-2)
=scaleb-op2	scaleB second operand is not an in-range integer (non-integer, out of range, or a NaN); the core takes an integer exponent
=over-precision	the operand has 35 significant digits: it exceeds decimal128 precision and rounds to exactly 1 on input, so log10 is 0, whereas decNumber computes log10 of the unrounded operand

